.TH COREGLOWCTL 1 "2026-11-01" "coreglow 0.9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
coreglowctl \- control Coreglow lighting and fan profiles from the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B coreglowctl
[\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B coreglowctl
lists and switches the profiles that
.BR coreglowd (1)
runs on a Corsair iCUE Commander Core XT (or, experimentally, a Commander CORE), shows live temperatures and fan
speeds, and changes a few settings. Changes apply within a frame; the
profiles themselves live in
.IR ~/.config/coreglow/config.toml .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Profile in use, temperatures, fan duties and speeds (the default command).
While an automatic rule is in charge, also the rule and the profile chosen by hand.
.TP
.B list
All profiles; \fB*\fR marks the one chosen by hand.
.TP
.BI set " NAME"
Use profile \fINAME\fR.
.TP
.BI brightness " NAME N"
Set the main lighting brightness of profile \fINAME\fR to \fIN\fR percent.
.TP
.B test-fans
Run the radiator fans at full speed for 5 seconds.
.TP
.BR auto " [" on | off ]
Show the automatic switching rules, or turn automatic switching on or off.
.TP
.BR motherboard " [" on | off ]
Send the lighting to the motherboard too, through an OpenRGB server.
.TP
.BR night " [" off " | \fIFROM\fB-\fITO\fR [\fIN\fR]]"
Show night dimming, turn it off, or dim all lighting to \fIN\fR percent (default 30)
between \fIFROM\fR and \fITO\fR, given as HH:MM (e.g. 22:00-07:00).
.TP
.B devices
What's connected to each RGB port: the layout in use, and what the controller
detected when
.B coreglowd
started (ports 1\(en6; the Core XT's external port isn't auto-detected), and which
controller it is.
.TP
.B edit
Open the profiles file in \fB$EDITOR\fR (nano by default) and check it afterwards.
.TP
.B path
Print where the profiles file is.
.TP
.B fallback
Store the backup fan curve in the controller. Only while
.B coreglowd
is stopped; it does this by itself otherwise.
.TP
.B gui
Open the window,
.BR coreglow (1).
.SH LAYOUT
The window edits this table too (Lighting, What's connected). Without a
.B [layout]
table the built-in layout is used. A table lists the devices on each port in
chain order, and may name parts:
.PP
.nf
[layout]
external = ["rgb-strip", "rgb-strip"]
port1 = ["ql-fan", "ql-fan"]
names = { fans = "port1", strips = "external" }
.fi
.PP
Device ids: rgb-strip, strip-15, strip-21, strip-27, strip-40, strip-49,
strip-84, triangle-9, ll-fan, ml-fan, hd-fan, 8-led-fan, ql-fan, pump-cap.
.PP
On a Commander CORE the ports are \fBpump\fR (capellix-pump or lcd-pump-ring) and
port1\(enport6 (fans only). Without a table its layout is what the controller detects.
Name selectors: a port ("port1"), a device on it ("external2"), a range of its
LEDs ("external:0-9"), joined with "+". This is experimental.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/coreglow/config.toml
Profiles, automatic rules, backup curve, motherboard sync.
.TP
.I ~/.config/coreglow/active
Name of the profile chosen by hand.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/coreglowd.sock
Control socket of
.BR coreglowd ;
used for set, test-fans and status when it's there.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/coreglow-status.json
Live values written by
.B coreglowd
every second.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
coreglowctl set rainbow
coreglowctl auto on
coreglowctl brightness white 40
coreglowctl night 22:00-07:00 30
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR coreglowd (1),
.BR coreglow (1)
